## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline materials formed by coordinating metal ions or clusters and organic ligands. MOFs have great application potential in chemical sensing [1,2], catalysis [3,4], drug delivery [5,6], hydrogen adsorption/separation [7], and energy storage [8] because of their customizable composition and porous structure. Furthermore, as compared to their pristine counterparts, MOF-based heterojunctions created by integrating functional nanomaterials, such as noble nanoparticles (NPs) [9], carbon materials [10], and transition metals oxide (TMO) NPs [11], exhibit enhanced and novel properties in many catalytic processes. Among these functional nanomaterials, TMO NPs have the most extensive application prospects in various fields owing to their high earth abundance, nontoxicity, low cost, and superior redox behavior. Studies have shown that TMO@MOF heterojunctions, such as $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}@$ FeBTC [12], $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ quantum dots@ZIF-8 [13], $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}{\scriptstyle-\mathrm{NP@MOF-7}4}$ [14], and $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ NP@ZIF-8 [15], demonstrate outstanding performances in electronic, optical, and electrochemical energy conversion and storage. However, current ${\mathrm{TMO@MOF}}$ heterojunction preparation approaches, such as the template and post-synthetic encapsulation approach, have some inadequacies, such as complexity, rigorous synthesis conditions, and poor controllability [16,17]. Thus, it’s essential to develop a simple, mild, and controllable approach for synthesis of TMO@MOF heterojunctions and further investigation of their properties.
Considering that MOFs contain transition metal sites, an intuitive thought to prepare TMO@MOF heterojunctions is partially destroying the MOF structures and directly transforming the destroyed counterparts into TMOs. Although extreme conditions including high temperature, strong oxydic, acidic or alkaline media can destroy MOFs, such destruction makes it difficult to regulate TMO growth [18]. By comparison, the electrochemical conversion is relatively mild and controllable. By adjusting the electric field conditions, it is desirable to synthesize TMO@MOF heterojunctions with regulatable nanostructures. These adjustable TMO@MOF nanostructures can lead to tunable electrocatalytic activity towards $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , which is a fundamental signal molecule in vivo. It is reported that cancers are closely associated with an abnormal level of intracellular $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production [19]. Up to now, few studies are focusing on in situ electrochemical conversion.
To shed light on this issue, this study proposes a simple electroreduction approach for the synthesis of Cu-based TMO@MOF heterojunction for the electrochemical sensing of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Benefiting from the high conductivity in electrochemical catalysis activity, the two-dimensional (2D) graphene-like conductive CuHHTP MOF $(\mathrm{HHTP}=2,3,6,7,10,11$ -hexahydroxytriphenylene) was selected as the study’s target MOF. We have used the copper hydroxide nanoarrays grown in situ on the copper foam (CF) as a template to in situ assemble as 2D conductive CuHHTP nanoarrays by epitaxial growth. Because $C u\mathrm{-}0_{4}$ nodes are distributed periodically in the Cu-based MOF, $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs with uniform distribution are generated in situ using simple electro-reduction (Fig. 1). Interestingly, the size and quantity of the generated $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs could be freely modulated by the applied potential and reduction time, which would further affect the electrocatalytic performance. Another fascinating discovery is that the heterojunction materials generated by electroreduction show remarkable electrocatalytic activity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . This substantial improvement of the catalytic performance lies in the ‘‘Fenton-like” reaction of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs. $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP heterojunction combined the high catalytic activity of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with the porous structure and conductivity of CuHHTP, which was suitable for application as an electrochemical $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ biosensor. Thus, the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP heterojunction nanoarrays used in this study showed outstanding $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection performance with tremendously high sensitivity $(8150.6~{\upmu\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{mM}}^{-1}{\cdot}{\mathrm{cm}}^{2})$ , a low detection limit $(5\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~M})$ , and satisfactory selectivity. The sensor also successfully detects $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the serum and urine.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. $C u(O H)_{2}/C F$ preparation
A piece of copper foam (CF) $(0.5\:\mathrm{cm}\times0.5\:\mathrm{cm}$ ) was successively washed with HCl, acetone and deionized water to remove the oxide and oil stain on the surface. An aqueous solution with $0.1\mathrm{~M~}(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}$ and $2.5\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ was prepared. Then the clean Cu foam was dipped into the above solution for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ which was oxidated to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ . Subsequently, it was cleaned with water and dried in an oven. The obtained blue precursor is $\mathsf{C u}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}/\mathsf{C F}$ .
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{C u}+4\mathsf{N a O H}+(\mathsf{N H}_{4})_{2}S_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}\to\mathsf{C u}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}+2\mathsf{N a}_{2}S\mathsf{O}_{4}+2\mathsf{N H}_{3}}\\ {\uparrow+2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}}\end{array}
$$
## 2.2. CuHHTP/CF preparation
The CuHHTP/CF was synthesized by an epitaxial growth method. Firstly, $10~\mathrm{mg}$ HHTP was added into $5~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol. The solution was then poured to a $10~\mathrm{mL}$ hydrothermal reactor after stirring for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Afterwards, the prepared $\mathsf{C u}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}/\mathsf{C F}$ was immersed into the above methanol solution and maintain $80~^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The acquired black CF was then cleaned with absolute ethyl alcohol and dried in air, denoting as CuHHTP/CF.
## 2.3. $C u_{2}O$ NP@CuHHTP/CF preparation
The electroreduction of CuHHTP/CF was conducted in a threeelectrode system. The electrolyte was a mixture of $0.5~\mathrm{M}$ KCl and $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ phosphate buffered saline (PBS) with a pH of 7.4. Pt wire was used as auxiliary electrode and $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ was applied as reference electrode. The reduction was operated at $-1\mathrm{~V~}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . For comparison, $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF were prepared with different potential (-0.3, 0.4, 0.6, $-0.8$ and $-1.2\mathrm{V}$ ) and different reducing time (0 min, 10 min, $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , 30 min, 120 min and $240~\mathrm{{min}}$ ).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphological and structural characterization
The morphological change of CF is characterized by fieldemission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). The blue appearance of CF observed from Figure S1A to S1B indicate the successful epitaxial growth of $\operatorname{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ on the surface of CF after soaking of pristine CF into sodium hydroxide and ammonium persulfate solutions. The formed $\operatorname{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ exhibits the smooth morphology of nanoarray with the length and diameter of $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2A and 2D). Additionally, the rough and loose microporous structure is displayed in Fig. 2B and 2E, accompanying by the significant appearance color changes of the blue Cu foam (Figure S1C). This demonstrates the formation of CuHHTP after the reaction of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ arrays with HHTP ligands. Finally, the CuHHTP sample is subjected to appropriate electrochemical reduction (Figure S1D) for obtaining $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF heterojunctions, of which the roughness reduction of the nanoarray is attributed to partial decomposition of CuHHTP at a certain potential (Fig. 2C and 2F). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) is employed to further evaluate the size and distribution of the generated $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs. The typical TEM observation of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP (Fig. 2G) reveals that a large number of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs with an average diameter of about $4\ \mathrm{nm}$ are uniformly distributed in the nanoarrays. Moreover, the relationship between the evolution of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs with the applied reduction potentials is also systematically discussed. As shown in Figure S6A-S6E, the diameter of the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs decreases from about $100\mathrm{nm}$ to $4\mathrm{nm}$ as the potential increase from $-0.4$ to $-1\vee\thinspace\mathsf{v S}\thinspace\mathsf{A g}/$ AgCl, which results from the faster growing of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs at higher reduction potential. In this size range of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs, the sensitivity of the sensor is significantly negative correlated with the size of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs because of the denser active sites exposed by the smaller $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs [20,21]. Worth mention, the skeleton of CuHHTP is destroyed at the high reductionpotential of $-1.2\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure S7), and almost all of CuHHTP nanoarrays are reduced to $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ . Highresolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) measurement is adopted to accurately observe the microstructure of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP. As exhibited in Fig. 2H, the obvious lattice distance of $0.251~\mathrm{{nm}}$ matches the (1 1 1) plane of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ . The elementary composition of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP is confirmed initially via Energy Dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDX). Fig. 2I displays the corresponding results of element mappings, where the symmetrical element signals indicate the uniform distributions of Cu, C and O elements over the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP sample.
The phase variation during the sample preparation is characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD). Fig. 3A shows that the diffraction peaks at 2h of $16.6^{\circ}$ , 23.9, $33.7^{\circ}$ , $35.6^{\circ}$ , $38.4^{\circ}$ , $39.7^{\circ}$ , $43.6^{\circ}$ , $53.2^{\circ}$ and $56.2^{\circ}$ are the peaks of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ . After reaction with HHTP ligands, the diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{Cu(OH)}_{2}$ disappear, and three new peaks at $9.78^{\circ}$ , $12.87^{\circ}$ and $27.69^{\circ}$ are observed, which are corresponding to the diffraction peaks of CuHHTP [22,23]. This data demonstrates the successful synthesis of CuHHTP. After the electrochemical reduction, the peaks of CuHHTP decrease, and new peaks at $29.5^{\circ}$ , $36.5^{\circ}$ and $42.3^{\circ}$ appear, which are associated with $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ (JCPDS no. 05-0667). It indicates that part of $C u\mathrm{-}O_{4}$ sites in CuHHTP are destroyed and in situ converted into $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ , meanwhile the rest CuHHTP retains the frame structure. The residual CuHHTP can serve as porous and conductive frames, which can significantly enhance the charge and mass transfer [24]. The valence variation of the Cu atoms is investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure S2 and Fig. 3B). It’s shown in Fig. 3B that there is one dominating peak at binding energy of $934.4\mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ species and one weak peak at 933.1 eV for ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ or ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ state in CuHHTP, which is in line with the reports of CuHHTP [25]. After electrochemical reduction, the peak of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ decreases while the peak of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ or ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ increases vastly. To distinguish ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ or ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ , we investigate the Auger spectrum of $\mathtt{C u}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaCu}$ LMM). Fig. 3C shows the $\mathtt{C u}$ LMM, which exhibits the band at $570.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ attributed to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $569.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ . There is no peak for ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ at $567.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , suggesting no ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ is reduced to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{0}$ . In order to determine how much of CuHHTP is transformed into $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs, the integration of $\mathtt{C u2p}$ $3/2$ for ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ are calculated, respectively (Fig. 3B). The result shows that the content of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ decreases from $84\%$ to $55\%$ after the electrochemical treatment, indicating $29\%$ of the CuHHTP is reduced to ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}$ . Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) is also employed to characterize the materials (Fig. 3D). In HHTP, two peaks at 1656.715 and $3417.577{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ belong to the hydroxyl groups. These two peaks disappear in CuHHTP, but appear in $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP, indicating that many hydroxyl groups of the uncoordinated HHTP ligands are again exposed in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{NP@CuHHTP}$ . Therefore, the FTIR results agree with the XRD, XPS and Auger spectrum analysis. $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ sorption measurements indicate that $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP still possesses a porous structure. The Brunner-Emmet-Teller (BET) surface $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP remains $166.09~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure S3A) with micropore size of $1.84~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S3B), which is in accordance with the hexagonal arrangement of one-dimensional pores (Fig. 1).
## 3.2. Electrochemical behavior of $C u_{2}O$ NP@CuHHTP/CF
To investigate conductivity of the materials, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) has been performed in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ PBS $\left(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\right)$ (Fig. 4A). The Randle equivalence circuit is displayed in the inset of Fig. 4A, in which $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ is the charge transfer resistance. As the results of EIS fitting exhibited in Table S1, bare CF displays a small $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $55.2~\Omega$ . After the chemical oxidation of copper foam, the $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ of $\mathsf{C u}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}/\mathsf{C F}$ rapidly increases to $413.2\Omega$ . With the formation of conductive CuHHTP, the $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ of CuHHTP/CF nanoarrays decreases to $162.8~\Omega$ . After the electrochemical treatment, although part of the conductive CuHHTP is destroyed, the $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}0\mathrm{NP@CuHHTP/CF}$ is $201.5\Omega$ , suggestive of relatively good conductivity. Fig. 4B compares the typical CVs of the aforementioned electrodes for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M PBS $\mathrm{(pH=7.4)}$ containing $1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ . According to the measured peak current for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction, the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF shows much higher activity over other investigated electrodes. Furthermore, we also compare the CVs of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}0~{\mathrm{NP@CuHHTP/CF}}$ with and without of $1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ . The peak current of the CV with $1\mathrm{\mM}\mathrm{\H}_{2}{\mathrm O}_{2}$ is about three times higher than that of CV without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , indicating the respose of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ ${\mathsf{N P@C u H H T P/C F}}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . To obtain various electron-transfer parameters of $\mathrm{Cu_{2}O N P@C u H H T P/C F_{\cdot}}$ CV is carried out in $0.1~\mathrm{{M}}$ PBS $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ -saturated) containing $1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ at different potential scan rates. It is shown in Fig. 4C and 4D that the peak currents increase with the raising of scan rates $(5{-}70\mathrm{mV}/s)$ and are in linear with the square root of the scan rates. This discovery indicates the fast electron transfer kinetics on $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF and a diffusion-controlled process.
## 3.3. Electrochemical sensing performances of $C u_{2}O$ NP@CuHHTP/C
To acquire the linearity range for electrochemical detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , amperometric response of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is tested (Fig. 5A), which is performed by the successive adding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with a certain concentration gradient in 0.1 M PBS at $-0.30\mathrm{V}.$ . The recorded current responds extremely fast to the variations in the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and reaches the steady state within $5s$ after each addition. The inset shows that the sensing linearity range for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is from $100\mathrm{nM}$ to $6.661\ \mathrm{mM}$ with the sensing sensitivity and limit of detection (LOD) of $8150.6~{\upmu\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $0.05~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (signal–noise ratio, $S/\mathsf{N}=3$ ), respectively. Notably, the electrochemical performance of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection has surpassed that of most reported Cu-based materials, such as $\mathsf{N C@C u_{x}0}$ [26], CuO nanoflowers [27], $\mathtt{C u C o_{2}O_{4}}$ [28], $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{-r G}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{-P}$ [29] and $\mathtt{C u_{x}O}$ NPs@ZIF-8 [15] (Table S2). Besides, the sensing sensitivity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}0~{\mathrm{NP@CuHHTP/CF}}$ is higher than that of most non-noble metal-based MOF composites and is also comparative with noble metal-based MOF composites (Table S3).
The catalytic mechanism in this work is researched with comparison experiments, in which the as-prepared CF, $\mathsf{C u}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}/\mathsf{C F}$ , CuHHTP/CF and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}0~{\mathrm{NP@CuHHTP/CF}}$ are tested by amperometric i t curve with the adding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 5B, the current response of bare CF and $\mathsf{C u}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}/\mathsf{C F}$ are negligible. And the current response of CuHHTP/CF increases obviously because of the porous structure of the MOF. Interestingly, the current response of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF increases dramatically up to five times that of CuHHTP/CF, when part of the CuHHTP is electrochemically reduced to $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ . The results indicate that $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP is much more active for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction. This result is in consistent with the previous reports that ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ has strong oxidability rather than reducibility [30]. And the catalytic mechanism of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP is further investigated. The hydroxyl radical generation and consumption of CuHHTP/CF and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}0\ N\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{CuHHTP}/\mathrm{CF}$ are tested by adding $100~\mathrm{{mM}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and subsequent applying of $-0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ potential after certain time. Fig. 5C shows that the hydroxyl radical concentration increases significantly after adding $100\mathrm{\mM}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and decreases after applying a negative potential of $-0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ in $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF system, while there is negligible hydroxyl radicals in CuHHTP/CF system, indicating that the catalytic reduction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF involves the ‘Fenton-like’ reaction $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ can react with $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ and produce hydroxyl radicals [31,32]. Hydroxyl radicals have strong oxidability, and they can easily obtain electrons and be reduced to $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , thus amplification the response to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . In addition, it has been reported that $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ is not only the catalytic active site but also the adsorption site of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [33]. Therefore, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules are firstly adsorbed by $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs. Then with $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ as catalyst, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules are decomposed and generate two hydroxyl radicals (OH). subsequently, the hydroxyl radicals (OH) are further reduced to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The electrocatalytic mechanism of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ electroreduction can be explained with following equations:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{\texttt{Cu}_{2}O}+2\mathrm{\texttt{H}_{2}O}_{2}\to2\mathrm{\texttt{CuO}}+2\cdot\mathrm{\texttt{OH}}+\mathrm{\texttt{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\\ &{\mathrm{\texttt{Cu}}_{2}\mathrm{O}+2\cdot\mathrm{\texttt{OH}}\to2\mathrm{\texttt{CuO}}+\mathrm{\texttt{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\\ &{2\mathrm{\texttt{CuO}}2\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{\texttt{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}\to\mathrm{\texttt{Cu}}_{2}\mathrm{O}+2\mathrm{OH}^{-}}\end{array}
$$
the overall reaction is:
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow20\mathrm{H}^{-}
$$
The anti-interference performance of this sensor is investigated by successively adding $0.1~\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $1\mathrm{\mM}\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$ , $1~\mathrm{mM}$ Uric Acid (UA), $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ glucose (GL), 1 mM l-cysteine (L-Cys), $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ ascorbic acid (AA), $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ dopamine (DA), $1\ \mathrm{mM\Fe^{3+}}$ and $0.1\mathrm{\mM\H_{2}O_{2}}$ at $-0.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Fig. 5D). It’s obvious that compared with the addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the current response generated by the addition of other small molecules is negligible. These results show that $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ ${\mathsf{N P@C u H H T P/C F}}$ exhibits good selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ detection. This may be due to the low operating potential $(-0.3{\mathrm{~V}})$ , which can restrict the electrochemical reaction of other substances. We also test the reproducibility of this sensor and find that the relative standard deviation (RSD) is less than $4\%$ with seven different electrodes (Fig. 5E, Inset). The storage stability of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}0\mathrm{NP@CuHHTP/CF}$ is also tested. Before its usage after a period of storage, the electrode must be reactived by $-1\mathrm{~V~}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Fig. 5E shows that after storage for 7 days at room temperature, the attenuation of current response is only $14.9\%$ . These results verify the high stability and reproducibility of the proposed biosensor. We further inspect the operation stability of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF. We use this sensor to measure $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration continuously for $1800\ s.$ . After $1800\ s_{\scriptscriptstyle}$ , the response maintains $86.3\ \%$ of the initial response (Figure S8). Furthermore, the sensitivity of the electrode remains basically unchanged even after the next two rounds.
To verify the feasibility of the sensor in detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in biological environment, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in diluted serum samples is determined by standard addition method. Table S4 shows that the prepared sensor has a good response to the injection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into PBS diluted serum, and the detection results are in consistent with the actual values. Therefore, the fabricated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ sensor based on $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF reveals broad application prospect in the analysis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in biological samples.
## 3.4. Cancer diagnosis of $C u_{2}O$ NP@CuHHTP/CF in serum
Cancer has become an important cause of death of the world’s population and a serious threat to human life and health. In this work, we choose the undilute urine and serum of healthy person and serum of patients with gastric carcinoma, Breast cancer patients and patient with lung cancer for real-time sensing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to display the detecting performance of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF (Fig. 5F) in real biological samples. The aliquots of serums and urine are injected into $0.1~\mathrm{~M~}$ stirring PBS $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ and then $25\mathrm{\mM\H_{2}O_{2}}$ concentrations is subsequently added. The amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the biological samples is calculated with the regression equation and shows that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations of urine and serum of healthy person were 3.2 and $7.4~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and serum of patients with gastric cancer, breast cancer patients and lung cancer are 17.5, 14.8 and $13.3~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively. It should be noticed that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration in the serum of cancerous person is significantly higher than that of healthy person. Cancer cells can release much more $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ than normal cells and these $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can diffuse through cell membranes freely [34,35], leading to the obviously increase of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ level in serum. These results fully confirm the reliable application of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ monitoring in biological fluids.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have proposed a facile and mild electrochemical reduction method to synthesize $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP heterojunction nanoarrays. By partly decomposing the conductive MOF CuHHTP, part of $\mathsf{C u-O_{4}}$ nodes in the MOF are converted to uniformly distributed $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs. We have discovered the size of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs can be effectively regulated from 100 to $4\mathrm{nm}$ via changing the applied potential from $-0.4$ to $-1\vee$ . In this size range, the sensitivity of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP heterojunction nanoarrays increase with the decreasing of the size of the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NPs. Arising from the high catalytic activity of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the porous structure and conductivity of CuHHTP MOF, coupled with the positive effect of the ordered nanoarray structure, the resultant $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ NP@CuHHTP/CF exhibits enhanced electrocatalytic properties with a low LOD $(5~\times~10^{-8}~\mathrm{~M~})$ , an extensive linear range $\mathrm{'}100\ \mathrm{nM}\mathrm{-}6.661\ \mathrm{mM}\mathrm{)}$ , striking sensitivity $(8150.6~{\upmu\mathrm{A}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mM}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-\bar{2}})$ , preeminent stability and anti-interference, exceeding most of the reported Cu based materials and MOF composites and even surpassing some noble metal catalyst. The sensor also shows potential in early cancer detection based on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in serum and urine. We profoundly envision this in situ electrochemical reduction approach provides a promising guidance for the synthesis and application of MOF based heterojunctions, but also inspires to deeply explore the mechanism of defect formation in MOFs.
